<ArticleLayout
  title="What to Eat When You Have the Flu"
  description="Foods and drinks that support recovery from influenza. What to eat, what to avoid, and when appetite loss becomes a concern."
  publishDate="July 2026"
  lastReviewed="July 2026"
>
  <p>
    When you have influenza, eating may be the last thing on your mind. Fever, fatigue, nausea, and loss of appetite are all normal flu symptoms — your body is directing energy toward your immune system rather than digestion. But nutrition still matters. The right foods and fluids, in the right amounts, can support recovery. This guide covers what to eat (and what not to), why hydration is more important than food during the acute phase, and when appetite loss signals a problem.
  </p>

  <h2>Acute Phase (Days 1–4): Hydration First, Food Second</h2>
  <p>
    During the acute phase of influenza — when fever is highest, body aches are worst, and appetite is minimal — <strong>hydration is the priority</strong>. The body can tolerate a day or two of very low caloric intake, but even mild dehydration impairs immune function and worsens symptoms. Fever increases insensible fluid loss through sweating and rapid breathing; every 1°C rise in body temperature above normal increases metabolic water requirements by about 10%.
  </p>

  <h3>What to Drink</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Water</strong> — plain, room temperature or warm (cold liquids can irritate a sore throat). Sip steadily throughout the day.</li>
    <li><strong>Oral rehydration solutions</strong> (Pedialyte, DripDrop, WHO oral rehydration salts) — these are superior to water for maintaining hydration status because the glucose-sodium co-transport mechanism maximizes fluid absorption. They are particularly important for young children, older adults, and anyone with high fever or vomiting.</li>
    <li><strong>Herbal teas</strong> — ginger tea for nausea, peppermint tea for throat comfort, chamomile for its mild sedative effect to support sleep. All provide fluid plus some symptom relief.</li>
    <li><strong>Clear broths</strong> (chicken, vegetable, bone broth) — provide fluid, electrolytes (sodium, potassium), and a small amount of protein and calories.</li>
    <li><strong>Diluted fruit juice</strong> (50:50 juice to water) — provides glucose and some electrolytes without the osmotic load of full-strength juice.</li>
    <li><strong>Electrolyte-rich options:</strong> coconut water (natural source of potassium), oral rehydration popsicles (useful when nausea makes drinking difficult).</li>
  </ul>

  <div class="callout-warning">
    <strong>⚠️ What to avoid:</strong> Caffeinated beverages in large amounts (mildly diuretic; one cup of coffee or tea is fine), alcohol (dehydrating, interacts with medications, impairs immune function), and very sugary drinks like soda or undiluted juice (can pull water into the gut and worsen diarrhea).
  </div>

  <h2>When Appetite Returns: The Best Foods for Flu Recovery</h2>
  <p>
    As the fever breaks and appetite begins to return (typically days 3–5), the goal is to provide easily digestible, nutrient-dense foods that support immune function without overwhelming a digestive system that has been largely dormant.
  </p>

  <h3>The BRAT Diet and Its Rationale</h3>
  <p>
    The BRAT diet — <strong>B</strong>ananas, <strong>R</strong>ice, <strong>A</strong>pplesauce, <strong>T</strong>oast — is a time-tested approach for gastrointestinal illnesses, and it applies to flu recovery as well. These foods are:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Low in fiber</strong> — easy on a resting digestive system</li>
    <li><strong>Binding</strong> — helpful if diarrhea is present (which can occur with influenza, especially in children)</li>
    <li><strong>Bland</strong> — unlikely to trigger nausea</li>
    <li><strong>Starchy</strong> — provide readily available energy (glucose) for immune cells, which are metabolically demanding</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    <strong>Important context:</strong> The BRAT diet is a starting point, not a complete nutritional plan. It is low in protein, fat, and many micronutrients. As your appetite improves, expand beyond these four foods. The BRAT diet is most useful for the first 24–48 hours of reintroducing food.
  </p>

  <h3>Foods That Support Immune Function</h3>
  <table class="prose">
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Food</th><th>Why It Helps</th><th>How to Eat It When Sick</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><strong>Chicken soup</strong></td><td>Provides fluid, electrolytes, protein, and cysteine. Research from the University of Nebraska found it inhibited neutrophil migration (reducing inflammation in airways).</td><td>Warm broth with small pieces of chicken and soft vegetables. Sip the broth first, eat solids as tolerated.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>Eggs</strong></td><td>High-quality protein with all essential amino acids needed for antibody production. Also contain zinc and selenium (immune-support minerals).</td><td>Scrambled or soft-boiled — easiest to digest. Avoid fried eggs, which can be harder on the stomach.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>Yogurt (plain, with live cultures)</strong></td><td>Provides protein and probiotics. Evidence suggests the gut-lung axis plays a role in respiratory immunity. Easier to digest than solid protein sources.</td><td>Plain yogurt; avoid high-sugar flavored varieties. If dairy mucus is a concern for you, note that this is largely anecdotal — no strong evidence links dairy to increased mucus production.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>Oatmeal / porridge</strong></td><td>Provides complex carbohydrates for sustained energy. Warm and soothing for a sore throat. Contains beta-glucan, which has immune-modulating properties.</td><td>Cook with water or milk (see dairy note above). Add a small amount of honey for taste and cough relief.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>Bananas</strong></td><td>Potassium (lost through sweating), vitamin B6 (immune function), and easily digestible carbohydrates. Gentle on the stomach.</td><td>Eat ripe bananas whole or mashed. Can be mixed into oatmeal or yogurt.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>Sweet potato / mashed potato</strong></td><td>Complex carbs, potassium, vitamin A (important for mucosal immunity in the respiratory tract). Soft and easy to eat.</td><td>Boiled or baked and mashed. Avoid heavy butter, cream, or cheese if the stomach is sensitive.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>Ginger</strong></td><td>Anti-nausea effects supported by multiple clinical trials (commonly used for chemotherapy-induced nausea and motion sickness). Anti-inflammatory properties.</td><td>Fresh ginger tea (steep sliced ginger in hot water 10 minutes), or small amounts of crystallized ginger.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>Citrus fruits (in moderation)</strong></td><td>Vitamin C supports several immune cell functions. The acidity may irritate a sore throat, so choose mandarins or oranges over grapefruit.</td><td>Whole fruit preferred over juice (fiber slows sugar absorption). If throat is very sore, diluted juice or juice popsicles.</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h2>Foods to Avoid During Flu Recovery</h2>

  <h3>High-Fat and Fried Foods</h3>
  <p>
    Fatty foods slow gastric emptying (stomach emptying into the small intestine). When nausea is already present, this can make things worse. Fried foods also trigger the release of cholecystokinin (CCK), a hormone that can increase nausea in sensitive individuals. Save the comfort-food cheeseburger for when you're genuinely recovered.
  </p>

  <h3>Spicy Foods</h3>
  <p>
    Capsaicin (the compound that makes chili peppers hot) can irritate an already inflamed throat and may trigger coughing. While spicy food can temporarily clear nasal passages (the "wasabi effect"), this is a short-lived effect and comes at the cost of throat irritation.
  </p>

  <h3>High-Sugar Foods and Drinks</h3>
  <p>
    Large amounts of refined sugar can cause rapid blood glucose spikes followed by crashes that worsen the fatigue you're already experiencing. High sugar intake may also transiently suppress neutrophil function — the white blood cells that are actively fighting the infection. A small amount of sugar for palatability is fine; a pint of ice cream is not.
  </p>

  <h3>Very Cold Foods and Drinks</h3>
  <p>
    Ice-cold beverages and frozen treats can temporarily worsen throat pain and trigger cough in an already sensitive airway. Room temperature or warm is better during acute illness. Cold popsicles can be helpful if they're the only way to get fluids in (especially for children), but they shouldn't be the primary hydration source.
  </p>

  <h3>Alcohol</h3>
  <p>
    Alcohol is dehydrating, disrupts sleep architecture (you may fall asleep faster but get less restorative deep sleep and REM sleep), impairs immune function (even a single episode of heavy drinking reduces natural killer cell activity), and interacts dangerously with acetaminophen — the combined liver stress of alcohol and acetaminophen metabolism can cause liver injury at doses below the normal acetaminophen toxicity threshold. <strong>Do not drink alcohol while you have the flu.</strong>
  </p>

  <h2>When Appetite Loss Is Concerning</h2>
  <p>
    Reduced appetite for 2–4 days during acute influenza is normal and not dangerous for otherwise healthy adults. The body shifts metabolic resources away from digestion and toward the immune response — this is adaptive, not pathological. However, certain situations require attention:
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>Complete inability to eat or drink for more than 24 hours:</strong> This risks dehydration and calorie deficiency, particularly in children and older adults. Seek medical care if fluids cannot be kept down.</li>
    <li><strong>Appetite loss persisting beyond the acute illness:</strong> If appetite hasn't begun to return by day 5–7 after symptom onset, this is unusual and should be discussed with a healthcare provider.</li>
    <li><strong>Weight loss:</strong> Some weight loss during a febrile illness is expected (mostly water weight from dehydration). Weight loss exceeding 5% of body weight in a short period, or continued weight loss after the fever resolves, warrants evaluation.</li>
    <li><strong>Signs of significant dehydration:</strong> No urination in 8+ hours, dark urine, dizziness when standing, sunken eyes, confusion, or in infants, a sunken fontanelle (soft spot). These require immediate medical attention.</li>
    <li><strong>Vomiting:</strong> While mild nausea is common with flu, repeated vomiting that prevents oral intake is less common and raises concern for dehydration. If vomiting persists for more than 24 hours, seek medical care.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Special Populations</h2>

  <h3>Children</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Dehydration risk is higher in children due to their higher body water percentage and higher metabolic rate. Monitor fluid intake carefully.</li>
    <li>Oral rehydration solutions (Pedialyte) are preferable to water, juice, or sports drinks. Sports drinks (Gatorade) have too much sugar and too few electrolytes for medical rehydration.</li>
    <li>Breastfeeding or formula feeding should continue for infants with flu. Breast milk provides antibodies and immune factors that support the infant's immune response.</li>
    <li>Do not force a sick child to eat. Fluids are the priority. Appetite returns naturally as the illness resolves.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Older Adults (65+)</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>The thirst mechanism becomes less sensitive with age. Older adults may not feel thirsty even when significantly dehydrated — proactive fluid intake is essential.</li>
    <li>Nutritional reserves may be lower at baseline, making adequate caloric intake more important than in younger adults.</li>
    <li>Medications (particularly diuretics for blood pressure) can compound dehydration risk. Continue prescribed medications unless instructed otherwise by a healthcare provider.</li>
    <li>Protein intake is particularly important to prevent muscle loss (sarcopenia) during the illness. Eggs, yogurt, nutritional supplement drinks (Ensure, Boost), and soft fish are good options.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Pregnant Women</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Fever during pregnancy, especially in the first trimester, should be treated with acetaminophen (which is pregnancy-safe) to reduce potential risk to the developing fetus.</li>
    <li>Hydration needs are higher in pregnancy. Aim for clear urine as a hydration marker.</li>
    <li>Food safety is paramount: avoid raw or undercooked foods, unpasteurized dairy, and deli meats (always important in pregnancy, but especially when the immune system is already stressed).</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Sample Meal Plan for Recovery Phase (Days 3–7)</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Breakfast:</strong> Plain oatmeal with a drizzle of honey and half a banana. Cup of ginger or chamomile tea.<br/>
    <strong>Mid-morning:</strong> Small cup of chicken broth or oral rehydration solution if hydration is still a concern.<br/>
    <strong>Lunch:</strong> Scrambled egg with plain toast. Applesauce cup.<br/>
    <strong>Afternoon:</strong> Yogurt (plain, with live cultures) or a small bowl of chicken soup.<br/>
    <strong>Dinner:</strong> Mashed sweet potato with a small portion of poached or baked chicken. Additional broth.<br/>
    <strong>Before bed:</strong> Warm herbal tea with 1–2 teaspoons of honey (for cough).
  </p>

  <h2>Key Takeaways</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Hydration is more important than food during the acute phase (first 2–4 days). Oral rehydration solutions are superior to plain water.</li>
    <li>Start with BRAT-adjacent foods (bananas, rice, applesauce, toast, oatmeal, broth) when appetite returns. Expand to include protein (eggs, yogurt, chicken) as tolerated.</li>
    <li>Avoid alcohol, high-fat/fried foods, excessive sugar, and very spicy foods during recovery.</li>
    <li>Appetite loss for a few days is normal. Inability to keep fluids down for 24+ hours, signs of dehydration, or appetite loss persisting beyond the acute phase need medical attention.</li>
    <li>Children and older adults have higher dehydration risk and require more vigilant monitoring.</li>
    <li>Listen to your body — cravings for specific foods during recovery often reflect genuine nutritional needs.</li>
  </ul>
